**Driving ports** (inbound or primary ports) define what the application can do — its use cases. In Java, a driving port is an interface in the domain package: `PlaceOrderUseCase`, `CancelSubscriptionPort`. No framework annotations, no HTTP concepts, no messaging concepts.

**Driving adapters** are the concrete entry points that call those ports. A REST controller is a driving adapter. A GraphQL resolver is a driving adapter. A Kafka consumer processing incoming commands is a driving adapter. Each translates from its specific protocol into a call on the domain's port.

```java
// PORT — in the domain package, zero framework imports
public interface PlaceOrderUseCase {
    OrderResult execute(PlaceOrderCommand command);
}

// ADAPTER — in the infrastructure.web package, depends on the port
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/api/orders")
public class OrderRestAdapter {
    private final PlaceOrderUseCase placeOrder;

    @PostMapping
    public ResponseEntity<OrderResponse> create(
            @Valid @RequestBody CreateOrderRequest request) {
        OrderResult result = placeOrder.execute(request.toCommand());
        return ResponseEntity.status(HttpStatus.CREATED)
                .body(OrderResponse.from(result));
    }
}
```

The REST adapter knows nothing about how orders are placed — it calls the port. Tomorrow, you can add a GraphQL resolver or a CLI command that calls the same `PlaceOrderUseCase`. The business logic is written once and accessed from every entry point. The adapter handles protocol translation; the port handles business execution.

**Driven ports** (outbound or secondary ports) are interfaces the domain defines to describe resources it needs from the outside world. The domain says "I need to persist an order" without specifying how. It says "I need to send a notification" without naming Kafka or email.

**Driven adapters** provide concrete implementations. A JPA adapter persists to PostgreSQL. A Kafka adapter publishes to a topic. In tests, a mock adapter returns canned data with zero infrastructure.

```java
// PORT — in domain.port.out, zero infrastructure imports
public interface OrderPersistencePort {
    Order save(Order order);
    Optional<Order> findById(OrderId id);
}

// ADAPTER — in infrastructure.persistence
@Repository
public class JpaOrderAdapter implements OrderPersistencePort {
    private final JpaOrderRepository jpaRepo;

    @Override
    public Order save(Order order) {
        OrderEntity entity = OrderMapper.toEntity(order);
        OrderEntity saved = jpaRepo.save(entity);
        return OrderMapper.toDomain(saved);
    }
}
```

The mapping between JPA entity and domain object happens **inside the adapter.** The domain's `Order` is a plain Java object with business methods and no annotations. The JPA `OrderEntity` with `@Entity`, `@Column`, and `@OneToMany` lives entirely in the adapter package. Database schema changes — column renames, table splits, index additions — are contained within the adapter. Business logic code never sees them.

Spring's constructor injection wires adapters to ports at runtime. The use case declares `OrderPersistencePort` in its constructor; Spring injects `JpaOrderAdapter`. In tests, you pass a `MockOrderAdapter`. The domain code is identical in both environments.

The driving side consists of actors that initiate interactions with your application. A REST controller, a CLI tool, a scheduled job, a test harness — each is a **primary adapter** that translates external input into calls on an **input port**.

The input port is an interface defined inside the application core. In a Spring Boot system, `PlaceOrderUseCase` is an input port. A `@RestController` is a primary adapter that receives HTTP, converts it to a `PlaceOrderRequest`, and calls `useCase.execute(request)`.

```java
@RestController
public class OrderController { // primary adapter
    private final PlaceOrderUseCase useCase; // input port

    @PostMapping("/orders")
    public ResponseEntity<OrderDto> place(@RequestBody CreateOrderDto dto) {
        var response = useCase.execute(mapper.toRequest(dto));
        return ResponseEntity.status(201).body(mapper.toDto(response));
    }
}
```

The key discipline: controllers never contain business logic. If you see `if/else` domain branching in a controller, that logic belongs in the interactor behind the port. The adapter's only job is translation — HTTP to domain types on the way in, domain types to HTTP on the way out.

The driven side handles everything the application needs from the outside world: databases, message brokers, external APIs, file systems. The application core defines **output ports** — interfaces like `OrderRepository`, `PaymentGateway`, `NotificationSender` — and the infrastructure layer provides **secondary adapters** that implement them.

```java
public interface PaymentGateway { // output port — in application core
    PaymentResult charge(Money amount, PaymentMethod method);
}

@Component
public class StripePaymentAdapter implements PaymentGateway { // secondary adapter
    private final StripeClient stripe;

    @Override
    public PaymentResult charge(Money amount, PaymentMethod method) {
        ChargeRequest req = StripeMapper.toChargeRequest(amount, method);
        ChargeResponse res = stripe.charges().create(req);
        return StripeMapper.toPaymentResult(res);
    }
}
```

The application core has no idea Stripe exists. If you switch to Adyen tomorrow, you write a new `AdyenPaymentAdapter`, update the Spring configuration, and the interactor runs unchanged. This is not theoretical — it's how payment processor migrations actually work in production without touching business logic.

For testing, implement the same port with a `FakePaymentGateway` that records charges in a list. Tests verify business behavior without any network calls.
